  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CDT TUE SEP 1 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals a stout upper level\nridge expanding across the southeastern US as Tropical Depression\nEdouard continues to move slowly northwest across eastern Texas.\nIncreasing subsidence will help maintain dry conditions across\nthe area through the majority of the work week. With that said,\nwe will continue with low (10-20%) chances for diurnally driven\nconvection as a healthy plume of moisture remains in place across\nthe region.  We are not expecting much in the way of severe\nweather as wind shear will be minimal. However, instability will\nbe sufficient enough to support a stronger storm or two as we\nreach peak heating. Low to medium (20-50%) chances for showers\nand storms return to the forecast for the southern half of the\nstate late Thursday through the weekend as a shortwave rotates\nwest along the southern periphery of the ridge.\n\nOur increasing heat risk will be the main story over the next\nweek. High temps will settle into the mid to upper 90s daily with\nheat indices pushing into the 105-108 degree range. This will\npromote a major to extreme heat risk for parts of Central Alabama\ndaily as we head into the weekend. There were no changes made\nthis evening regarding our current Heat Advisory. It remains in\neffect from 11 AM Wednesday morning through 8 PM Saturday\nevening. We continue to emphasize the importance of hydration and\nlimiting outdoor exposure to mitigate the significant heat risk\nexpected throughout the week.\n\n95/Castillo",
